"Arranges ordered response categories around a central reference to compare the balance of opposing responses.\n\nUse when: Survey response options have a meaningful order and opposing directions. Readers need the balance of disagreement and agreement across questions.\n\nAvoid when: Response categories are unordered or the chosen center has no substantive meaning.\n\nVisual grammar: Segment length encodes response count or share. Left/right placement encodes response direction, with neutral placement explicitly declared.\n\nBuild: Preserve the response order from most negative to most positive. Choose and state how neutral responses are centered or separated. Compute cumulative positions around zero and label questions outside bars.\n\nCheck: Check all response categories and nonresponses are accounted for. Verify each question uses the declared denominator. Confirm the neutral split does not count any response twice.\n\nPitfalls: The zero line is a response-balance reference, not absence of responses. Do not imply that distances between ordinal response options are measured numeric intervals.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic agreement on five policies using five ordered response segments and respondent totals for each question.\n\nAlternatives: 100% stacked bar chart, Dot plot, Population pyramid."
